12 months of amenorrhoea, Vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes), Vaginal atrophy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-021
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of generalized anxiety and sleep deprivation secondary to intractable night sweats.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute psychological decompensation, severe palpitations, and an absolute inability to sleep for 72 hours due to unremitting, severe vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes and night sweats). Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out an acute cardiac event or thyroid storm, and to provide immediate short-term symptomatic relief to prevent severe sleep deprivation and mental health deterioration before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My heart is racing so fast I feel like I am having a heart attack, and these constant hot sweats mean I have not slept in three days.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have been progressing over the last year, marked by exactly twelve consecutive months of amenorrhoea without a pathological cause. The patient, aged 51, reports a prior history of progressively irregular menstrual cycles (perimenopause). Tonight, she describes severe vasomotor instability manifesting as frequent, intense hot flashes and drenching night sweats occurring every 30 minutes. This has caused profound sleep disruption and consequent daytime fatigue, culminating tonight in acute mood alterations, severe irritability, and an overwhelming anxiety/panic state. She also reports a one-year history of genitourinary syndrome characterized by vaginal dryness and dyspareunia, alongside decreased breast fullness.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently impaired by acute sleep deprivation and anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; acute psychological distress and exhaustion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, visibly flushed, diaphoretic, and highly anxious.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia, regular rhythm, no murmurs. Respiratory exam is clear. Thyroid is not palpably enlarged, no tremor or exophthalmos. Genitourinary exam (external) confirms signs of marked vaginal atrophy (pale, thinned mucosa). Abdomen is soft, non-tender.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom windows open, multiple fans running; environment indicates severe temperature intolerance.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took over-the-counter valerian root with zero efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Menopausal Vasomotor Exacerbation with secondary acute anxiety/insomnia - Status: Acute crisis
Differentials:

Hyperthyroidism / Thyroid Storm - Unlikely given the absence of goiter, tremor, or weight loss, and the classic 12-month history of amenorrhoea.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out clinically by the absence of chest pain, shortness of breath, or typical radiation; palpitations are clearly linked to the onset of the hot flashes.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - The anxiety is a secondary, acute consequence of severe physiological sleep deprivation and hormonal fluctuation, rather than a primary psychiatric disorder.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to extreme exhaustion)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute physical and psychological crisis driven by severe, intractable menopausal vasomotor symptoms. The objective clinical evidence, including sinus tachycardia clearly linked to observed hot flashes, a classic 12-month history of amenorrhoea, and observable vaginal atrophy, strongly supports a severe menopausal flare rather than acute cardiac or thyroid pathology. The profound level of sleep deprivation (72 hours) and resultant psychological decompensation required immediate after-hours intervention to safely provide short-term anxiolytic/sedative medication. Failing to break this cycle tonight would have resulted in an inevitable, highly distressed Emergency Department presentation for suspected cardiac symptoms or psychiatric crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral clonidine 25mcg to immediately blunt the centrally-mediated vasomotor instability. Prescribed a short course (3 days) of oral temazepam 10mg strictly for sleep induction to break the insomnia cycle. Advised on arranging formal laboratory testing (FSH/Estradiol) and discussing Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) with her regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically cool the body during a night sweat, keep a frozen gel pack under your pillow and apply it directly to the back of your neck when a flash starts; experiencing the rapid reduction in core temperature will reinforce immediate, physical symptom control. To improve sleep architecture, completely eliminate caffeine after 12:00 PM; noting the reduction in nighttime palpitations will ensure strict dietary compliance.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days to initiate long-term management, likely Hormone Replacement Therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the palpitations become continuous and irregular, if you develop central crushing chest pain, or if you feel overwhelmingly suicidal or unsafe.

